(i) **Replication fork.** The diagram shows the parental DNA double helix opening into a Y-shaped fork. Labels a marker looks for:
- **Template DNA (parental strands)**, the two separated strands with their 5' and 3' ends marked;
- an arrow showing the **direction of replication** (the fork moving into the unopened DNA);
- **Continuous synthesis** on the template with polarity 3'\(\rightarrow\)5', producing the **leading strand**;
- **Discontinuous synthesis** on the template with polarity 5'\(\rightarrow\)3', producing short stretches, the **Okazaki fragments**, that make up the **lagging strand**;
- the **newly synthesised strands**, each running antiparallel to its template and growing in the 5'\(\rightarrow\)3' direction.

(ii) DNA polymerase can add nucleotides only in the 5'\(\rightarrow\)3' direction. So:
- the **leading strand** is synthesised **continuously**, on the template strand with polarity 3'\(\rightarrow\)5', in the same direction as the fork moves;
- the **lagging strand** is synthesised **discontinuously**, on the template strand with polarity 5'\(\rightarrow\)3', as short Okazaki fragments that are later joined by DNA ligase.

(iii) Enzymes of DNA replication (any two): **DNA-dependent DNA polymerase**, which polymerises the new strand; **DNA ligase**, which joins the Okazaki fragments; **helicase**, which unwinds the double helix; **topoisomerase**, which relieves the strain of unwinding; and **primase**, which lays down the RNA primer.

(iv) **Vicia faba** (faba bean). Taylor and colleagues used radioactive thymidine to follow the distribution of newly synthesised DNA in its chromosomes in 1958, proving that replication is semi-conservative in a eukaryote as well.
